# Server construction Data Flow Diagram (DFD) :

## Legend
The goal of this document is to lay out a Class's field construction as
extensively as possible by representing how data is transformed in the code.

Since this is only broad clarification, some data is irrelevant to it, usually
things that are being left out are items out of the scope of the main data
transform, like sub features.
For example if a file involves a custom parser that is also a complex construction, it could involve a file like that, for now i just assume it can do what it does here.

This document is read as an ordered list of Nodes that each represent a variable
and its origins in a vertical graph fashion. (note: this format could be easily
transcribed to obsidian for a node view)

Nodes are represented in the following fashion:

<node_name>
type: [the literal type of the variable]
deps: [other variables (nodes) used to produce this one]
trans: [single function used to extract the variable (can be documented in parenthesis)]
brief: [a brief description]
docstring: [docstring]
</node_name>

Optionally every field can be on the next line, allowing for carriage returns.

## target Server fields

<clientPoll>
<gameQuiz>
<scoreboard>

## starting nodes

<argc>
<argv>

## preliminary transformations

<argc>
type: int
deps: /
trans: /
brief: /
docstring: /
</argc>

<argv>
type: char **
deps: /
trans: /
brief: /
docstring: /
</argv>

<serverArgs>
type: ServerArgs
deps: argv, argc
trans: ServerArgs(argc, argv)
brief: Contains the server args
docstring: Contains the server args as described in the readme, obtained as
           argument of the Server::Server()
</serverArgs>

## intermediary transformations

<playerCap>
type: size_t
deps: serverArgs
trans: serverArgs.getPlayerCap()
brief: max players in the game
docstring: /
</playerCap>

<port>
type: size_t
deps: serverArgs
trans: serverArgs.getPort()
brief: open port of the game socket
docstring: /
</port>

<configFileName>
type: std::string
deps: serverArgs
trans: serverArgs.getConfigFileName()
brief: config file name
docstring: /
</configFileName>

<clientPoll>
type: ClientPoll
deps: playerCap, port
trans: ClientPoll(playerCap, port)
brief: poll container
docstring:
Contains:
-   the poll
-   the lobby: during construction, a cli listens on stdin for `kick` and
    `start`; `start` finishes the construction. Admin commands are only
    available in the lobby, never during the game loop.
-   std::vector<std::string> this->getPlayerNames() -> extracts the player names
-   ~CLientPoll() ->    a destructor that cleanly notifies players of the end of
                        the game
</clientPoll>

<gameQuiz>
type: GameQuiz
deps: configFileName
trans: GameQuiz(configFileName)
brief: structured info from the toml
docstring:
Structured info from the toml, it contains:
-   std::vector<QuizQuestion> -> the quiz questions
-   AnswerEvaluator answerEvaluator class {
        TimeType lastQuestionStartTime -> the start time of the last question
        char lastCorrectAnswer -> last question's answer
        Scoreboard evalAnswers(TBD) ->  turns a batch of answers into a
                                                score increment
    }
-   Scoreboard evalAnswers(TBD) -> transmits the arg to answerEvaluator
-   Scoreboard nextQuestion(ClientPoll) ->  increments the question index by one
                                            displays the question accordingly
                                            and polls for delay seconds to get
                                            the answers, and then evaluates them
                                            (this is going to invlolve several
                                            more sub-classes and perhaps a
                                            nextQuestion_class_DFD.md)
                                            every wait (3s countdown, 5s
                                            question display, answer delay) is
                                            done by polling, never by sleeping
-   Scoreboard nextQuestion() ->    overload for `--test-verbose`: no
                                    ClientPoll is built, the question is dumped
                                    and the delays are printed instead of
                                    waited
-   bool quizIsOver() -> returns true if the quiz has reached the end
-   ostream& operator<<(ostream& ostream, const GameQuiz& quiz) -> dumps the
    config (used by `--test-verbose`)
</gameQuiz>

<playerNames>
type: std::vector<std::string>
deps: clientPoll
trans: clientPoll.getPlayerNames()
brief: a list of the player names
docstring: /
</playerNames>

<scoreboard>
type: Scoreboard
deps: playerNames
trans: Scoreboard(playerNames)
brief: The scoreboard of the game
docstring:
The scoreboard for the game, tracks the player trhough each game,
It contains the following:
-   std::vector<PlayerScore>[PlayerScore is a struct with std::string
    playerName and size_t playerScore] -> initializes the score for the players
    to 0.
-   ostream& operator<<(ostream& ostream, const Scoreboard& score) -> displays
    the scoreboard like in the README.md.
-   Scoreboard& operator+=(Scoreboard& destScore,
                           const Scoreboard& scoreIncrement)
    ->  sums two scoreboards (only works when the scoreIncrement contains a
        subset or all of the players of the destScore).
Kicked or disconnected players stay in the scoreboard, their score simply stops
increasing.
Ties:
-   ranking: tied players are ordered alphabetically.
-   podium: for each place, from first to third, a tie is broken by
    secretJuryDecision (the spelling bee jury): the player with the most B's in
    their name gets the place. If that is still a tie, the place is decided by
    std::hash<std::string> of the names (predictable, so it can be tested).
</scoreboard>

## usage of the taget server fields (in Server::run())

<scoreBoardIncrement>
type: Scoreboard
deps: gameQuiz, clientPoll
trans: gameQuiz.nextQuestion(clientPoll)
brief: contains the increment to the player score for a question
docstring:
calculate the increment to the player score using the player's answers (through
poll). For each PlayerAnswer:
when playerAnswer == lastCorrectAnswer
then score increment formula is:
((MIN_SCORE_INCREMENT - MAX_SCORE_INCREMENT) * x) / delay + MAX_SCORE_INCREMENT
where x is the time the player took to answer after the answers were shown.
/!\ a player's score is a size_t, this calculation should be rounded to the
    unit.
Answer rules:
-   the scoring clock starts when the answers are shown; answers sent before
    that are silently ignored.
-   answers sent after the end of the delay are not counted.
-   a player can change their answer until the end of the delay: the last
    valid answer counts (A then B then C -> C).
-   an invalid answer keeps the previous one (or no answer if there was none).
-   letters are case-insensitive: `a`-`d` and `A`-`D` are both valid.
-   no answer or a wrong answer earns no points.
</scoreBoardIncrement>
